  card="From the 6th century BCE rulers came to be known by titles such as rājā, mahārāja or samrāṭ. Early Indian texts give ideas of kingship: Kauṭilya's Arthaśhāstra says that only if a king is himself energetically active do his officers follow him energetically; the coronation oath in the Yajurveda advises the king to judge the strong and weak impartially and fairly, protect the country from calamities and do good to the people; the Śhānti Parva of the Mahābhārata guides rulers on ethical conduct, justice and responsibility to subjects. The king was primarily responsible for protecting his subjects from external threats and internal disorder and administering justice in cases of abduction, robbery, theft and adultery, with severe punishments for serious offences. Kingship was generally hereditary, but there are references to kings being elected or even expelled, so royal authority was not always absolute.",
